## Final Judgement
**Summary of Key Findings:**  
The fact-check reveals that the viral video shared by BJP MP Shobha Karandlaje was misrepresented. While the video is authentic footage from a Pakistani National Assembly session on October 26, 2020, featuring opposition members, the audio does not contain chants of "Modi, Modi." Instead, fact-checkers (including Alt News and FactHunt) confirmed the slogans heard were "voting-voting," related to parliamentary demands for a vote. The claim was debunked prior to its sharing, and no independent Pakistani media or officials verified the "Modi" chant narrative.

**Information Lacking:**  
No further information is needed; the evidence clearly addresses the core claim.

**Conclusion:**  
The fact-check explicitly refutes the claim that the video shows Pakistani opposition MPs chanting "Modi, Modi" inside their Parliament. The audio analysis and context demonstrate the slogans were misidentified, and the claim has been debunked by multiple fact-checking sources. Therefore, the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The viral video was misrepresented; fact-checkers [Alt News](https://www.altnews.in/were-modi-modi-slogans-raised-in-pakistan-parliament-india-tv-tv9-bharatvarsh-misreport/) and [FactHunt](https://facthunt.in/posts/1484/Truth-behind-the-viral-picture-showing-French-Teacher-who-was-beheaded-for) confirmed the slogans heard were "voting-voting," not "Modi-Moodi," as opposition Pakistani MPs demanded a vote during a parliamentary session. The claim was debunked prior to its sharing, with no independent Pakistani media verification of the "Modi" chant narrative, leading to a refuted verdict.